# .env.example — the source-of-truth list of environment variables this repo needs.
#
# HOW THIS WORKS
#   • This file is committed. It documents EVERY variable the project reads, with a
#     comment saying what it is and where to get it — but never a real value.
#   • Put real values in the git-ignored `.env` of the primary Git worktree. A
#     linked worktree keeps the committed example on its active branch but uses
#     that one durable live file; see wiki/development/secrets.md.
#   • When you add a variable in code, add it here too (blank, documented). This file
#     is the checklist a new contributor works down to get running.
#   • Rotating an existing value changes only the ignored live file unless the
#     variable's name, purpose, or sourcing guidance changed too.
#
# This convention is stack-neutral. Rename `.env`/`.env.example` to whatever your
# stack expects (e.g. a framework's own dotenv file) and keep the same discipline.

# --- Cloudflare (provisioning, deploys, and CI) ---

# USER-scoped API token. Create it under My Profile → API Tokens → Create Token
# → Create Custom Token (NOT the account's own API Tokens screen — a token made
# there can't reach the /user/* endpoints provisioning needs).
#
# Two permission rows, and one field people miss:
#     User    ▸ API Tokens ▸ Edit
#     Account ▸ API Tokens ▸ Edit
#     Account Resources: Include ▸ <your account>     ← don't skip this
#
# That's the whole ask — the agent grants itself Workers, D1, and anything else
# on demand (/verify's browser is a local CLI and needs no Cloudflare grant).
# Full walkthrough: wiki/stack/cloudflare-credentials.md
# Treat it like a root password — never commit the filled-in value.
#
# The NAME of this variable is owned by wiki/stack/cloudflare-credentials.md.
# Don't rename it here: wrangler, scripts/cf-secrets.mjs, .github/workflows/
# deploy.yml, and /wong-cloudflare all read CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN, and a rename
# is a behavioural change, not a docs edit.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# Your Cloudflare account ID. Dashboard → Workers & Pages → right-hand sidebar.
# /wong-cloudflare fills this in for you once you've picked an account.
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=

# --- Cloudflare Access (only if you turned on the login wall) ---

# Service token — lets CI and scripts reach Access-gated preview URLs without a
# browser. Created under Zero Trust → Access → Service Auth.
# Also required by the staging walkthrough on an Access-protected repo: without
# these it reports UNKNOWN rather than screenshotting the login page and calling
# it green. See wiki/stack/staging-walkthrough.md
# CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID=
# CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Staging walkthrough (only if you want media in the PR comment) ---
#
# The walkthrough needs no variables beyond CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN above — adding
# playwright-core to your app is the whole opt-in, and the browser runs on
# Cloudflare Browser Run (wiki/stack/staging-walkthrough.md). These two are optional
# and only affect how evidence is presented: set both and screenshots render
# inline in the PR comment; leave them unset and it cites local paths, which is
# a rung down rather than a failure.
#
# An R2 bucket with public access turned on. WALK_MEDIA_BASE_URL is its public
# base — the r2.dev URL Cloudflare gives you, or your own custom domain.
# WALK_MEDIA_BUCKET=
# WALK_MEDIA_BASE_URL=

# --- Example entries (delete these; replace with your project's real variables) ---

# The base URL of the API this app talks to. Get it from your deploy dashboard.
# API_BASE_URL=
